00:00:13.716 --> 00:00:21.156
Good afternoon everyone. Welcome back to AWS Singapore Summit
2026. I hope you have a great refreshment earlier.

00:00:21.436 --> 00:00:25.796
My name is Dennis and with me I have Boon Leong
here from S3 Engineering.

00:00:25.996 --> 00:00:30.156
Before we dive into the topic today,
I would like to pick your brain a little.

00:00:30.156 --> 00:00:35.156
Tell us about the journey and how did
the idea of your product came about,

00:00:35.156 --> 00:00:39.916
and what inspired you to take
on the challenge of actually combating deep fakes?

00:00:40.676 --> 00:00:43.756
Okay. Thank you Dennis. I think this is a very good question.

00:00:44.356 --> 00:00:47.156
The original idea came about a few years ago.

00:00:47.196 --> 00:00:50.436
There were two events that actually triggered us to do this.

00:00:50.476 --> 00:00:53.036
The first event was actually the onset of Covid.

00:00:53.476 --> 00:00:59.076
If the audience back then, we will remember
there is a lot of misinformation going on about vaccines,

00:00:59.116 --> 00:01:05.236
But I think the eureka moment for us
will be the emergence of generative AI technology.

00:01:05.276 --> 00:01:13.956
The ChatGPT moment per se. What we realize in this particular
event is that generative AI technology is highly proficient

00:01:13.956 --> 00:01:20.996
in generating synthetic content that is very hard
to discern with a human eye. So what this means to us, right?

00:01:21.036 --> 00:01:26.756
If you think on the positive side,
AI to be used for productivity is good to do summarization and all.

00:01:26.796 --> 00:01:34.996
But on the flip side, a malicious actor can exploit the same
technology to generate deepfakes, to generate misinformation, content,

00:01:35.276 --> 00:01:37.836
etc.. So what we really set out to do,

00:01:38.636 --> 00:01:43.876
the problem statement we try to address is really
to be able to survey the online space to look

00:01:43.876 --> 00:01:47.556
at generative AI content that is potentially malicious.

00:01:47.876 --> 00:01:51.356
And then we monitor the spread of this information online.

00:01:51.356 --> 00:01:56.156
And last but not least, what we have wanted
to do is to be able to attribute the source.

00:01:56.156 --> 00:01:58.396
So source evaluation is key for us.

00:01:58.756 --> 00:02:04.876
For us, our point of view is to be able to identify
the techniques that is used to generate the deepfake content.

00:02:05.236 --> 00:02:08.916
But more importantly, to be able to support these four areas.

00:02:09.436 --> 00:02:16.756
What's most important for us is to be able to customize models
to be able to iterate fast, build fast, because tactics, techniques,

00:02:16.756 --> 00:02:19.556
and procedures are ever changing in this day and age.

00:02:20.676 --> 00:02:24.676
Thanks for your great insights.
And this leads me to my next slide here.

00:02:24.956 --> 00:02:31.076
Spending growth per AI model type.
A recent study by Gartner released in 2020 5th July,

00:02:31.556 --> 00:02:39.076
reveals that AI applications built with custom
models is growing twice than those using foundational models.

00:02:39.236 --> 00:02:46.476
That said, the usage of foundational models dominates
in absolute terms, but the key takeaway here is the trajectory.

00:02:46.516 --> 00:02:46.876
Here,

00:02:48.036 --> 00:02:51.636
the accelerating investment in custom models signals a clear shift.

00:02:51.916 --> 00:02:59.836
Organizations and teams are increasingly recognized that off
the shelf models alone aren't enough to truly differentiate.

00:03:00.076 --> 00:03:05.436
Teams are tailoring custom models
to their specific products, domains, and use cases.

00:03:05.836 --> 00:03:07.916
This trend underscores a critical point.

00:03:08.316 --> 00:03:14.516
Custom model development is becoming not just a nice to have,
but a strategic competitive edge.

00:03:15.036 --> 00:03:19.636
Similarly, like what you mentioned,
agile has their own custom model as well.

00:03:21.356 --> 00:03:24.716
You may ask me why. Why do we want to have custom AI models?

00:03:25.356 --> 00:03:27.356
I think the first and most obvious reason.

00:03:27.356 --> 00:03:30.756
Off the shelf models are general purpose use.

00:03:30.916 --> 00:03:37.356
They are designed to generate content,
not to solve highly sophisticated problems like countering deep fakes.

00:03:37.556 --> 00:03:44.236
Even the most powerful out-of-the-box models may not
deliver the precision and performance your use case demands.

00:03:44.876 --> 00:03:49.356
Second, cause when you are in the proof of concept stage,

00:03:50.516 --> 00:03:53.556
calling a foundational model may work.

00:03:53.916 --> 00:03:58.756
However, as you scale to production,
the economic shifts off the shelf.

00:03:58.756 --> 00:04:05.876
Foundational models can be increasingly expensive to run at scale,
and this is where a purpose custom built model shines.

00:04:07.596 --> 00:04:15.076
Third, imagine if you are in a highly regulated industries,
for example, aerospace, defense, healthcare, or finance.

00:04:15.396 --> 00:04:20.756
You are likely faced with strict data residency requirements,
privacy and compliance requirements.

00:04:21.076 --> 00:04:25.716
Relying solely on an external
foundational model may not meet those standards.

00:04:26.036 --> 00:04:31.436
Custom models give you the capabilities
to tailor to your end user requirements.

00:04:33.076 --> 00:04:39.956
Last but not least, here, imagine if your application is simply
a wrapper around a publicly available foundational model.

00:04:40.276 --> 00:04:43.716
You are probably offering the same capability as everyone else.

00:04:44.196 --> 00:04:48.396
There is no mode here. Custom models give you the competitive edge,

00:04:48.556 --> 00:04:54.516
especially as you start building authentic solutions
where the intelligence of the models directly translate

00:04:54.516 --> 00:04:56.876
to the value you ship to your customers.

00:04:57.676 --> 00:05:00.000
Now, a show of hands from the audience who is actually
building and using custom models here.

